{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_00_3zaBFKOqCKj3R9jH5ZWh8572","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":{\"format\":\"section\",\"path\":\"paper_secs/secs_effAttn/Hardware-Efficient_Attention_for_Fast_Decoding/Hardware-Efficient-Attention-for-Fast-Decoding.md\",\"section\":{\"type\":\"heading\",\"target\":\"3 Methodology::4 System Optimization: Asynchrony & Distributed Offset Calculation::4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization\"},\"valueText\":\"#### 4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization\\n\\nWe use two techniques to overlap compute and memory loading:\\n\\n- 1. Software pipelining: we load the next KV block while the current KV block is being used in computation. This classical technique (Lam, 1988) avoids having the tensor cores waiting for memory loading.\\n- 2. Warp specialization: we have separate warps performing memory loading with either TMA (tensor memory accelerator) or asynchronous copy (cp. async instruction), and separate warps performing matrix-multiply-accumulate (MMA). The former act as *producer* warps, while the latter act as *consumer* warps (Bauer et al., 2014). This is commonly used in matrix multiplication (Thakkar et al., 2023) and attention (Shah et al., 2024a). This decoupling simplifies the software pipelining, allowing the warp scheduler to overlap the memory loading and compute.\"}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44bb2e52-013c-49b0-8208-b4db8179c32c","uuid":"f1809706-0cff-4fef-a443-f010e936b083","timestamp":"2026-05-31T10:01:14.413Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":{\"format\":\"section\",\"path\":\"paper_secs/secs_effAttn/Hardware-Efficient_Attention_for_Fast_Decoding/Hardware-Efficient-Attention-for-Fast-Decoding.md\",\"section\":{\"type\":\"heading\",\"target\":\"3 Methodology::4 System Optimization: Asynchrony & Distributed Offset Calculation::4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization\"},\"valueText\":\"#### 4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization\\n\\nWe use two techniques to overlap compute and memory loading:\\n\\n- 1. Software pipelining: we load the next KV block while the current KV block is being used in computation. This classical technique (Lam, 1988) avoids having the tensor cores waiting for memory loading.\\n- 2. Warp specialization: we have separate warps performing memory loading with either TMA (tensor memory accelerator) or asynchronous copy (cp. async instruction), and separate warps performing matrix-multiply-accumulate (MMA). The former act as *producer* warps, while the latter act as *consumer* warps (Bauer et al., 2014). This is commonly used in matrix multiplication (Thakkar et al., 2023) and attention (Shah et al., 2024a). This decoupling simplifies the software pipelining, allowing the warp scheduler to overlap the memory loading and compute.\"}}","structuredContent":{"result":{"format":"section","path":"paper_secs/secs_effAttn/Hardware-Efficient_Attention_for_Fast_Decoding/Hardware-Efficient-Attention-for-Fast-Decoding.md","section":{"type":"heading","target":"3 Methodology::4 System Optimization: Asynchrony & Distributed Offset Calculation::4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization"},"valueText":"#### 4.1 Asynchrony with software pipelining and warp specialization\n\nWe use two techniques to overlap compute and memory loading:\n\n- 1. Software pipelining: we load the next KV block while the current KV block is being used in computation. This classical technique (Lam, 1988) avoids having the tensor cores waiting for memory loading.\n- 2. Warp specialization: we have separate warps performing memory loading with either TMA (tensor memory accelerator) or asynchronous copy (cp. async instruction), and separate warps performing matrix-multiply-accumulate (MMA). The former act as *producer* warps, while the latter act as *consumer* warps (Bauer et al., 2014). This is commonly used in matrix multiplication (Thakkar et al., 2023) and attention (Shah et al., 2024a). This decoupling simplifies the software pipelining, allowing the warp scheduler to overlap the memory loading and compute."}}}}
